Instead of reducing the active range by only one every time, this version remembers the last index where a swap happened. Everything after that index is already in order, so the next recursive call can stop there.

An empty slice and a slice with one value return unchanged. This code also keeps the required slice return value.

```go
func bubbleSort(nums []int) []int {
    var sortPart func(int)

    sortPart = func(end int) {
        if end <= 0 {
            return
        }

        lastSwap := 0
        for i := 0; i < end; i++ {
            if nums[i] > nums[i+1] {
                nums[i], nums[i+1] = nums[i+1], nums[i]
                lastSwap = i + 1
            }
        }

        if lastSwap == 0 {
            return
        }

        sortPart(lastSwap - 1)
    }

    sortPart(len(nums) - 1)
    return nums
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` in the worst case. It can do fewer comparisons when the unsorted part is small. Space complexity: `O(n)` for the recursive calls. The original slice is updated.

The helper handles one full pass and then calls itself for the part that is still unsorted. Every pass places one large value at the right boundary.

The empty array and one-element array need no work. The `changed` flag is useful because a pass with no swaps means the whole active part is already ordered.

```java
class Solution {
    private void sortPart(int[] nums, int end) {
        if (end <= 0) {
            return;
        }

        boolean changed = false;
        for (int i = 0; i < end; i++) {
            if (nums[i] > nums[i + 1]) {
                int temp = nums[i];
                nums[i] = nums[i + 1];
                nums[i + 1] = temp;
                changed = true;
            }
        }

        if (!changed) {
            return;
        }

        sortPart(nums, end - 1);
    }

    public int[] bubbleSort(int[] nums) {
        sortPart(nums, nums.length - 1);
        return nums;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` in the worst case. With an already sorted array, it takes `O(n)`. Space complexity: `O(n)` because of recursion. Sorting is done directly in the input array.

This version uses one recursive call for every adjacent comparison. When a pass reaches its boundary, the next call starts a new pass with a shorter boundary. It is less efficient in stack usage than the usual helper, but the recursive flow is easy to follow.

Dry run on `[2, 1, 3]`: compare `2` and `1`, swap to get `[1, 2, 3]`, then compare `2` and `3`. The next pass checks the smaller range and makes no changes.

For `[5]` or an empty vector, the boundary check returns immediately.

```cpp
class Solution {
private:
    void compareNext(vector<int>& nums, int index, int end) {
        if (end <= 0) {
            return;
        }

        if (index == end) {
            compareNext(nums, 0, end - 1);
            return;
        }

        if (nums[index] > nums[index + 1]) {
            swap(nums[index], nums[index + 1]);
        }

        compareNext(nums, index + 1, end);
    }

public:
    vector<int> bubbleSort(vector<int>& nums) {
        compareNext(nums, 0, static_cast<int>(nums.size()) - 1);
        return nums;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)`. Because the recursive calls remain connected across all comparisons, the worst-case stack space is `O(n^2)`. The vector is sorted in place.

This version makes both parts of bubble sort recursive. `_scan` checks adjacent pairs during one pass. When it reaches the active end, `_pass` starts the next pass with one fewer position.

Dry run for `[3, 1, 2]`: the first scan changes it to `[1, 2, 3]`. The next scan finds no swap, so the recursion stops.

```python
class Solution:
    def _scan(self, nums, index, end):
        if index >= end:
            return False

        changed = False
        if nums[index] > nums[index + 1]:
            nums[index], nums[index + 1] = nums[index + 1], nums[index]
            changed = True

        return self._scan(nums, index + 1, end) or changed

    def _pass(self, nums, end):
        if end <= 0:
            return

        changed = self._scan(nums, 0, end)
        if not changed:
            return

        self._pass(nums, end - 1)

    def bubbleSort(self, nums):
        self._pass(nums, len(nums) - 1)
        return nums
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` in the worst case. The recursive scan adds a call for each comparison, so the stack space is `O(n)` at a time. The list is modified in place.

A regular bubble sort pass moves the largest remaining value to the end. After that, the last position does not need to be checked again, so the recursive call works on a smaller part of the vector.

The `swapped` flag also stops early when a pass makes no changes. For example, `[1, 2, 3]` finishes after the first pass.

```cpp
class Solution {
private:
    void sortPart(vector<int>& nums, int end) {
        if (end <= 0) {
            return;
        }

        bool swapped = false;
        for (int i = 0; i < end; ++i) {
            if (nums[i] > nums[i + 1]) {
                swap(nums[i], nums[i + 1]);
                swapped = true;
            }
        }

        if (!swapped) {
            return;
        }

        sortPart(nums, end - 1);
    }

public:
    vector<int> bubbleSort(vector<int>& nums) {
        sortPart(nums, static_cast<int>(nums.size()) - 1);
        return nums;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` in the worst case, and `O(n)` when the input is already sorted because of the early stop. Space complexity: `O(n)` for the recursive call stack. The input vector is changed in place.

