## Intuition
Bubble Sort typically relies on two nested loops: an inner loop to swap adjacent elements and an outer loop to reduce the window of unsorted elements. To convert this into a purely recursive approach without any `for` or `while` loops, we can use the call stack to track our state. We map the inner loop to an index variable `i` and the outer loop to a boundary variable `n` representing the size of the unsorted portion.

## Approach
The helper function `recursion(a, i, n)` drives the sorting process:
1. **Base Case:** If `n <= 0`, the entire array has been sorted, and the recursion terminates.
2. **Inner Loop Simulation:** We compare the current element `a[i]` with the next element `a[i+1]`. If they are out of order, we swap them. We then advance to the next adjacent pair by recursively calling the function with `i + 1`.
3. **Outer Loop Simulation:** When `i == n - 1`, it means we have reached the end of the current unsorted window. The largest element of this pass has successfully "bubbled" to its correct position at the end. We then reset the index `i` back to `0` and shrink the unsorted boundary `n` by 1 to start the next pass.

## Solution
```cpp
class Solution {
private:
    void recursion(vector<int>&a,int i,int n)
    {
        if(n<=0)return;
        if(i==n-1)
        {
            recursion(a,0,n-1);
            return;
        }
        if(a[i]>=a[i+1])
        {
            swap(a[i],a[i+1]);
        }
        recursion(a,i+1,n);
    }
public:
    vector<int> bubbleSort(vector<int>& nums) 
    {
        recursion(nums,0,nums.size());
        return nums;
    }
};
```
