## Complexity
- **Time Complexity:** O(N^2) where N is the number of elements in the array. The algorithm performs the exact same number of comparisons as standard iterative Bubble Sort. The window shrinks by 1 each pass (N + (N-1) + (N-2) ... + 1), resulting in a quadratic time complexity. 

- **Space Complexity:** O(N^2) auxiliary stack space. Because both the outer and inner loops are implemented via continuous recursion, the maximum depth of the call stack before hitting the final base case will be proportional to the total number of operations (N * (N-1) / 2). 

*Note: Because the recursive calls are at the very end of the function execution paths, this is theoretically a tail-recursive function. A modern C++ compiler with Tail Call Optimization (TCO) enabled (e.g., `-O2` or `-O3`) will likely flatten this into a standard loop, bringing the actual runtime space complexity down to O(1).*

## Intuition
Bubble Sort works by repeatedly swapping adjacent elements if they are in the wrong order. Instead of using nested loops, we can use two recursive functions: an inner recursive function to push the largest element to the end of the unsorted segment, and an outer recursive function to reduce the unsorted range step-by-step.

## Approach
1. Inner Recursion (inner): Simulates the inner loop. It iterates through adjacent elements up to size - 1 using a index parameter j. If nums[j] > nums[j + 1], it swaps them and recursively calls inner(nums, size, j + 1).
2. Outer Recursion (outer): Simulates the outer loop. In each step i, it calls inner(nums, size - i) to place the current maximum element at its correct sorted position at the end. It then recursively calls outer(nums, size, i + 1).
3. Base Cases:
    - For inner: Stop when j == size - 1.
    - For outer: Stop when i == size - 1 (the entire vector is sorted).
    - For bubbleSort: If the vector size is $\le 1$, return directly.

## Solution
```cpp[]
class Solution {
public:
    void inner(vector<int>& nums, int size, int j = 0) {
        if (j == size - 1) return;

        if (nums[j] > nums[j + 1]) {
            swap(nums[j], nums[j + 1]);
        }

        inner(nums, size, j + 1);
    }

    void outer(vector<int>& nums, int size, int i = 0) {
        if (i == size - 1) return;

        // Pass (size - i) to avoid processing already sorted end elements
        inner(nums, size - i);

        outer(nums, size, i + 1);
    }

    vector<int> bubbleSort(vector<int>& nums) {
        if (nums.size() <= 1) return nums;
        outer(nums, nums.size());
        return nums;
    }
};
```

